---
import { ArrowLeft } from 'lucide-preact'

import { assetPath } from '@/lib/paths'
import Layout from '@/layouts/Layout.astro'
import '@/styles/global.css'

const pageTitle = 'Export & Quality Guide | Splicer'
const pageDescription =
  'How Splicer exports video: why exporting re-encodes, what WebM and the quality presets actually do, and how to avoid quality loss.'

const logoSrc = assetPath('brand/logo-mark.svg')
const homeHref = assetPath('')
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <main class="mx-auto flex w-full max-w-3xl flex-col gap-8 px-4 py-8 md:py-12">
    <header class="flex items-center justify-between gap-3">
      <a
        href={homeHref}
        class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-sm font-medium text-slate-600 transition-colors hover:bg-slate-100 hover:text-slate-900 hover:duration-0 dark:text-slate-300 dark:hover:bg-slate-800 dark:hover:text-slate-100"
      >
        <ArrowLeft class="h-4 w-4" />
        Back to editor
      </a>
      <a href={homeHref} class="flex items-center gap-2" aria-label="Splicer home">
        <img src={logoSrc} alt="Splicer logo" class="h-7 w-7" />
        <span class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Splicer
        </span>
      </a>
    </header>

    <article class="flex flex-col gap-6">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h1 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Export &amp; quality guide
        </h1>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
          A quick guide to what happens when you export, why a WebM file is not automatically
          smaller or higher quality, and how to keep as much quality as possible.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-lg border border-slate-200/70 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-700/60 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <p class="font-medium text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Exporting to WebM always re-encodes your video, which loses a little quality:
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Your source is already lossy. Its original codec (for example the H.264 in an MP4)
            discarded data when the video was first encoded.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Exporting to WebM decodes the video back to raw pixels, then re-encodes them with VP8 or
            VP9. That second encode is also lossy. This is called generation loss, like photocopying
            a photocopy.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            The only way to lose zero quality is to not re-encode at all (stream-copy). But H.264
            cannot live in a WebM container, so WebM export always re-encodes. There is no zero-loss
            path from an MP4 to WebM.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Exporting usually re-encodes your video
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          When you export to a different format than your source, Splicer decodes your video back
          to raw frames and encodes them again from scratch. Your source file is already
          compressed (and that first compression already discarded some detail), so this second
          encode is a fresh round of compression on top of it. That is the key thing to keep in
          mind: most exports are a re-encode, not a copy.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          WebM is a container, not a codec
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          A &ldquo;.webm&rdquo; file is just a box. The efficiency comes from the codec inside it,
          which for WebM is VP8 or VP9. Choosing WebM does not by itself make a file smaller or
          better. What matters is which codec you use and how it is configured.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Why &ldquo;smaller and lossless&rdquo; is not possible when re-encoding
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Re-encoding an already-compressed video is like photocopying a photocopy: each pass
          loses a little, an effect called generation loss. You cannot add back detail that was
          already thrown away, so a re-encode can never increase quality. Getting a smaller file
          with truly zero quality loss is not achievable by re-encoding. The only way to lose
          nothing is to not re-encode at all (see below).
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Is WebM actually more efficient?
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          It depends entirely on the codec, not the container:
        </p>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium">VP8</span> is roughly the same generation as the H.264 found
            in most MP4s, so it is often no smaller (and can be larger) than your source.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium">VP9</span> is meaningfully more efficient, comparable to
            H.265 / HEVC, and can produce smaller files at similar visual quality.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            <span class="font-medium">AV1</span> is more efficient still, but is out of scope for
            this app.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          So a smaller file generally means VP9, applied to a source that was not already encoded
          very efficiently, and accepting a small amount of generation loss.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          What the quality presets do
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          The Lossless, High, Medium, and Low presets set the target bitrate for the re-encode,
          from highest to lowest. More bitrate means better quality and a larger file. One
          important caveat: &ldquo;Lossless&rdquo; targets a very high bitrate for near-transparent
          quality, but it is not mathematically lossless. If your goal is to keep every pixel
          identical, a re-encode is not the way to do it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          How to export with no quality loss
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Export to the <span class="font-medium">same format as your source</span>, at
          <span class="font-medium">Lossless</span> quality, with FPS left on
          <span class="font-medium">Original</span> and without cropping or muting. Under those
          conditions Splicer copies the video stream directly instead of re-encoding it, so there
          is no quality loss and the export finishes almost instantly. Trimming is still allowed in
          this mode. Changing the format, changing the FPS, cropping, or muting a segment all force
          a re-encode and therefore some quality loss.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">
          Will exporting make my file smaller?
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          Only sometimes. If your source was encoded inefficiently (a high bitrate for its
          content) and you re-encode it with an efficient codec like VP9, you can get a smaller
          file that looks nearly identical. If your source is already well compressed, re-encoding
          it may make it larger, not smaller.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-2 rounded-lg border border-slate-200/70 bg-slate-50/60 p-4 dark:border-slate-700/60 dark:bg-slate-900/40">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-slate-50">Recommendations</h2>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-1.5 pl-5 text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Want zero quality loss? Export to the same format as your source at Lossless quality
            with Original FPS and no crop or mute.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Want a smaller file that looks the same? Use WebM with VP9 and accept a small,
            usually invisible, amount of generation loss.
          </li>
          <li class="list-disc leading-relaxed">
            Avoid VP8 if your goal is a smaller file; it rarely beats the H.264 in a typical MP4.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
